<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Empire of Japan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Imperial Japan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> was the Japanese state from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a> of 1868 until the post-war <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-japan">constitution of 1947</a>. It modernised rapidly, defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, acquired colonies including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, and expanded across East and South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> before its defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> in 1945.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan.jpg" alt="Empire of Japan" width="320" height="224" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: kahusi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Japan_(1870%E2%80%931999).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>After two centuries of the seclusion policy known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sakoku">sakoku</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> was forced open to trade when the American commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthew-c-perry">Matthew C. Perry</a> arrived in 1854 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/convention-of-kanagawa">Convention of Kanagawa</a> was signed. Commercial treaties with Western countries followed, and their humiliating terms turned many against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a>, giving rise to the radical movement <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sonno-joi">sonnō jōi</a>, Revere the Emperor, expel the barbarians. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boshin-war">Boshin War</a> power was restored to the emperor from the shōgun, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charter-oath">Charter Oath</a>, made public at the enthronement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-meiji">Emperor Meiji</a> on 7 April 1868, set out the aims of the new government and the legal stage for modernisation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Under the slogans Enrich the Country, Strengthen the Armed Forces and Promote Industry, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> went through large-scale industrialisation and militarisation, often regarded as the fastest modernisation of any country to date. It joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> on the Allied side in 1914, occupied the German-leased territory of Tsingtao and German islands in the Pacific, which in 1920 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> placed under Japanese administration as the South Seas Mandate. Hirohito came to the throne on 25 December 1926. During the 1920s the country moved toward a democratic system, but parliamentary government was not rooted deeply enough to withstand the economic and political pressures of the 1930s, when military leaders gained influence.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> in 1931 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mukden-incident">Mukden Incident</a> and left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> in 1933. Its forces won large successes in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-sino-japanese-war">Second Sino-Japanese War</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-war">Pacific War</a>, notably the attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attack-on-pearl-harbor">Pearl Harbor</a> on 7 December 1941, but from 1942, after Allied advances at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-midway">Midway</a> and Guadalcanal, Japan was on the defensive. After the dropping of atomic bombs on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiroshima">Hiroshima</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a> and the Soviet invasion of Manchuria, it accepted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam-declaration">Potsdam Declaration</a> on 14 August 1945, and the formal surrender was signed on 2 September.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The empire had three emperors, Meiji, Taishō and Shōwa. Its political system has been debated, since there was no single dictator, and terms proposed include militarism, totalitarianism and police state. Imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> committed widespread war crimes in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-sino-japanese-war">Second Sino-Japanese War</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing-massacre">Nanjing Massacre</a>, involving the deaths of millions. The Allied occupation under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douglas-macarthur">Douglas MacArthur</a> lasted until 1952, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-san-francisco">Treaty of San Francisco</a> took effect. A new constitution was ratified on 3 May 1947, ending the empire, and the armed forces were dissolved. The Japan Self-Defense Forces were created in 1954, and Okinawa was returned to Japan in 1972.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Empire of Japan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The institutions of the <b>Empire of Japan</b> were built on the Meiji Restoration, and the Meiji Constitution of 1889 remained in place until the Allied occupation after the Second World War.</blockquote><a href="/movement/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Negotiations between the <b>Empire of Japan</b> and the Kingdom of Joseon were concluded.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-26">26 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The battle ended the Satsuma Rebellion, the last internal mutiny in the <b>Empire of Japan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-24">24 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Meiji Japan</b> annexed Hokkaido in 1869 and pursued assimilation.</blockquote><a href="/state/ainu-chiefdoms">Ainu chiefdoms</a></li><li><blockquote>Its men, Kido Takayoshi, Ito Hirobumi and Yamagata Aritomo, led <b>Meiji Japan</b>; it became Yamaguchi Prefecture in 1871.</blockquote><a href="/state/choshu-domain">Choshu Domain</a></li></ul></section>
